---
import Layout from "@components/Layout.astro";
import PageHeader from "@components/PageHeader.astro";
import DecklistTooltip from "@components/DecklistTooltip.astro";
import { Card } from "webcoreui/astro";
---

<Layout title="Strategic Theory - Replenish Guide" description="Master the foundational macro-concepts of competitive Premodern Replenish. Learn to navigate dual-angle disruption, execute patient endpoint control, and maintain rigorous selection discipline.">
    <PageHeader
        title="Strategic Theory"
        description="Master the foundational macro-concepts that dictate competitive game flow—navigating bi-directional disruption vectors, executing patient endpoint control, and maintaining strict resource discipline."
        imageSrc="/src/assets/banners/decree.jpeg"
        imageAlt="Decree of Silence"
        imageCaption="Decree of Silence by Adam Rex"
        isCard={true}
    />

    <Card title="Strategic Frameworks" titleTag="h2">
        <h3 style="margin-top: 0;">The Two-Angle Disruption Framework</h3>
        <p>
            Parallax Replenish excels at insulating its game plan against single-axis strategies. Pure aggression, linear mana denial, or slow countermagic suites can all be systematically dismantled. True strategic tension emerges when an opposing archetype attacks across two intersecting angles simultaneously—such as an early aggressive clock backed by land destruction (<a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Wasteland">Wasteland</a>, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Rishadan Port">Rishadan Port</a>) or fast pressure supported by cheap permission.
        </p>
        <p>
            When confronting dual-angle pressure, your opening hand must contain early answers aligning precisely against their primary threat vector. Because dedicated selection engines begin at three mana, attempting to fix a misaligned starting hand across early turns is mathematically fatal. This principle must strictly dictate your mulligan discipline in volatile matchups: viable keepable hands must balance early interactive speed with an accessible pathway to an operational combo engine.
        </p>

        <h3>Endpoint Play vs. Opportunistic Transitions</h3>
        <p>
            Against heavy permission decks, evaluate game state progression through the lens of terminal endpoints. Imagine a hypothetical game state where both players draw out their entire libraries: if your ultimate counter-threat density overcomes their interaction count, you are statistically favored to win long attrition battles. In such matchups, resource preservation is paramount. Avoid executing speculative cantrips or discarding high-value cards for trivial tempo advantages—never activate <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Attunement">Attunement</a> without a documented plan for every card being discarded, and cast <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a> strictly during the opponent's end step.
        </p>
        <p>
            In protracted counter-wars, if a control opponent retains eight permission spells while you assemble four copies of <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a>, four <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Opalescence">Opalescence</a>, and three <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Decree of Silence">Decree of Silence</a>, your terminal spell will ultimately resolve—provided you have not frittered away redundant engine pieces in early exchanges.
        </p>
        <p>
            Post-sideboard, strategic dynamics alter significantly. With access to proactive disruptive tools like <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Defense Grid">Defense Grid</a>, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Meddling Mage">Meddling Mage</a>, and <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Exalted Angel">Exalted Angel</a>, you gain the capacity to engineer explosive early winning windows. Recognizing precisely when to abandon conservative endpoint play to force a rapid opportunistic lethal transition is a hallmark of elite Replenish mastery.
        </p>

        <h3>Attunement Discipline</h3>
        <p>
            A prevalent execution error among developing pilots is activating <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Attunement">Attunement</a> blindly without a definitive resource plan. Before resolving an activation, you must confidently answer two analytical criteria: which exact cards in hand are expendable, and what precise mechanism or answer is required from the top of the deck? If either question remains ambiguous, forego the activation. Particularly against interactive control structures, every reckless filtering loop risks stripping irreplaceable lands or protective redundancy, exposing your late-game posture to fatal single-point answers.
        </p>
    </Card>
    <DecklistTooltip />
</Layout>
